// NEGATIVE TEST -- N2-08. A `/*` that nothing closes is an error, and it says
// so.
//
// Comments nest, so "unterminated" is a real state the scanner can be in and
// distinguish: the `*/` below closes the INNER comment, leaving the outer one
// open to end of file. Under the old non-nesting regex the same file lexed as
// a closed comment followed by live source, and the trailing `*/` produced
// `Expected expression, but found '/'` -- a message about the wrong character
// on the wrong line.
//
// Treating the rest of the file as a comment instead would compile a SHORTER
// program than the author wrote, which is the outcome a deleted `*/` should
// never have.
fn main() {
    /* outer /* inner */
    print("never reached");
}
